{"units":[
{"id":"pkg/router/aggregate_discovery.go:newDiscoveredAggregateBackend","summary":"The discovered aggregate backend's ReverseProxy now sets FlushInterval to -1, so every write is flushed to the client at once. Responses with a Content-Length were previously buffered, so this changes streaming behavior for all aggregate traffic.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"Aggregate backend proxy now flushes after every write","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Changes runtime proxy flushing for all traffic through this backend.","risk_signals":["streaming/flush behavior changed"],"focus":["Flushing per write is acceptable for large non-watch responses with Content-Length","Same setting is applied consistently to all proxies"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/forward.go:NewForwardBackend","summary":"The forward backend's ReverseProxy now sets FlushInterval to -1, flushing every write immediately. Responses with a Content-Length were previously buffered, so this changes behavior for all forwarded traffic.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"Forward backend proxy now flushes after every write","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Changes runtime proxy flushing for all forwarded traffic.","risk_signals":["streaming/flush behavior changed"],"focus":["Flush per write has no performance regression for large list responses","Watch events arrive without delay through this proxy"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/proxy_errors.go:const streamingFlushInterval","summary":"Adds a constant of -1 used as ReverseProxy.FlushInterval, meaning flush after every write. It takes effect through the three proxies that use it, and the comment's claim holds for them.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"New streamingFlushInterval constant set to -1","confidence":0.8,"reason":"A constant only; the behavior change is in the proxy units that reference it.","risk_signals":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/st_fallback.go:type singleTenantFallback","summary":"The breakers cache now stores *groupBreaker, an alias for gobreaker's TwoStepCircuitBreaker, instead of the one-step breaker. This is a type change that supports reporting outcomes when the status is written.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"refactor","headline":"ST fallback breaker cache holds two-step breakers","confidence":0.75,"reason":"Type change following the alias; semantics are in the breaker.go changes.","risk_signals":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/st_fallback.go:newSingleTenantFallback","summary":"The LRU for breakers is now created with the groupBreaker element type. It is a type parameter change only.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"refactor","headline":"Breaker LRU constructed with groupBreaker type","confidence":0.85,"reason":"Type parameter change only, matching the struct field.","risk_signals":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/st_fallback.go:(*singleTenantFallback).forward","summary":"The single-tenant fallback proxy now sets FlushInterval to -1, so each write is flushed immediately instead of relying on ReverseProxy's no-Content-Length heuristic. This affects all ST fallback traffic.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"ST fallback proxy now flushes after every write","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Changes runtime proxy flushing for all single-tenant fallback traffic.","risk_signals":["streaming/flush behavior changed"],"focus":["Flushing per write is acceptable for non-watch responses","ModifyResponse origin check still runs before any flush"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/st_fallback.go:(*singleTenantFallback).breakerForDestination","summary":"The return type changes to *groupBreaker, matching the cache's new element type. The key and locking logic are unchanged.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"refactor","headline":"breakerForDestination returns groupBreaker type","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Signature type change only; callers must use the two-step Allow API, which is covered in the breaker changes.","risk_signals":[]}
]}

{"units":[
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker.go:type statusRecorder","summary":"Adds an unexported onStatus callback field to statusRecorder. It does nothing until WriteHeader invokes it, so the field alone is low risk.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"statusRecorder gains an optional onStatus callback field","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Unexported field, nil by default, and only the WriteHeader and serveThroughBreaker changes make it matter.","risk_signals":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker.go:(*statusRecorder).WriteHeader","summary":"WriteHeader now calls onStatus with the first final status (>=200 or 101) before forwarding it. This is what lets the breaker outcome be reported when headers are written instead of when the handler returns.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"WriteHeader reports final status to onStatus callback","confidence":0.75,"reason":"Runs breaker accounting inline on the response write path, so a reviewer must judge ordering and repeat calls.","risk_signals":["callback on response write path","breaker accounting timing"],"focus":["callback runs before the underlying WriteHeader, and the once guard in serveThroughBreaker covers repeated WriteHeader calls","implicit 200 via Write reaches WriteHeader, so onStatus still fires"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker.go:type groupBreaker","summary":"Introduces a type alias for gobreaker's two-step breaker, used wherever the per-group breaker is held. The alias is what moves the whole package onto the two-step API.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"refactor","headline":"Add groupBreaker alias for two-step circuit breaker","confidence":0.8,"reason":"A type alias adds no behavior; the real change is in newGroupBreaker and serveThroughBreaker.","risk_signals":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker.go:newGroupBreaker","summary":"The per-group breaker is now built with NewTwoStepCircuitBreaker, with the same settings. This changes how callers must report outcomes (Allow/done instead of Execute).","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"newGroupBreaker builds a two-step circuit breaker","confidence":0.7,"reason":"Changes the breaker's calling contract, so every user must report outcomes or hold half-open slots forever.","risk_signals":["breaker API contract changed"],"focus":["every caller of the breaker (including the ST fallback) now calls done exactly once","IsExcluded still applies to outcomes passed to done"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker.go:serveThroughBreaker","summary":"Replaces cb.Execute with Allow plus a done callback guarded by sync.Once. The outcome is reported at the first final status write, with a deferred fallback and panic handling, so a long watch no longer holds a half-open trial slot.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"Breaker outcome now reported when status is written, not on return","confidence":0.85,"reason":"Rewrites breaker accounting, concurrency (sync.Once) and panic handling on the main proxy path.","risk_signals":["concurrency","panic handling","error handling changed","breaker semantics"],"focus":["a backend that returns 200 and then fails mid-body is now counted as success","the panic path re-panics and still reports exactly one outcome","a handler that writes nothing reports rec.status 0 through breakerOutcome","a request that is not allowed returns 503 before withProxyFailure"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker_test.go:withGroupHandlerAndBreaker","summary":"Test helper signature changes to take the groupBreaker alias. No assertions change.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"test","headline":"Test helper takes groupBreaker type","confidence":0.9,"reason":"Test-only type update to follow the production type change.","risk_signals":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker_test.go:TestHandleFuncBreakerHalfOpenCapRejectsConcurrentTrial","summary":"Test now builds a two-step breaker to match the production type. Assertions are unchanged.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"test","headline":"Half-open cap test uses two-step breaker","confidence":0.88,"reason":"Test-only constructor swap, with no assertion removed or loosened.","risk_signals":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker_test.go:tripBreaker","summary":"The helper trips the breaker through Allow and done instead of Execute. If Allow fails, it silently does nothing.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"test","headline":"tripBreaker uses Allow/done to force a failure","confidence":0.85,"reason":"Test-only helper updated for the two-step API with equivalent effect.","risk_signals":[]}
]}

{"units":[
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker_test.go:TestReconcileUnchangedKeyPreservesBreakerState","summary":"The test now builds its breaker with the two-step constructor to match the new groupBreaker type. Assertions are unchanged, so a line-by-line review can be skipped.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"test","headline":"Reconcile-preserves-state test uses two-step breaker constructor","confidence":0.9,"reason":"Test-only type adaptation to the new breaker type; no assertions removed or changed.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker_test.go:TestReconcileChangedKeyResetsBreaker","summary":"The test now builds the old breaker with the two-step constructor to match the new groupBreaker type. Assertions are unchanged, so a line-by-line review can be skipped.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"test","headline":"Changed-key reset test uses two-step breaker constructor","confidence":0.9,"reason":"Test-only type adaptation to the new breaker type; no assertions removed or changed.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/router.go:type handlerEntry","summary":"handlerEntry now holds the two-step breaker plus a per-handler watches context and its cancel func. reconcile cancels it when the handler is replaced or removed, so streaming watches end and clients re-watch.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"handlerEntry gains two-step breaker and per-handler watch context","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Adds struct fields and a cancellation lifecycle that changes how long-lived watch requests end.","risk_signals":["struct fields added","concurrency","context lifecycle"],"focus":["Places that build handlerEntry directly (tests omit watches/endWatches) tolerate nil watches","Every discarded entry is cancelled, so no context leaks"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/router.go:newHandlerEntry","summary":"New constructor creates a handlerEntry with a fresh breaker and a cancellable watches context. It replaces the inline struct literal and in-place mutation in reconcile.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"New newHandlerEntry constructor creates breaker and watch context together","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Creates cancellable contexts whose lifecycle governs when in-flight watches are terminated.","risk_signals":["context lifecycle","resource leak if never cancelled"],"focus":["endWatches is called on every path where an entry is discarded","Deriving the context from Background rather than the service context is intended"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/router.go:type servingEntry","summary":"servingEntry now carries the groupBreaker type and the handler's watches context so the request path can scope watches to the handler generation.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"servingEntry carries watch context and groupBreaker type","confidence":0.75,"reason":"Struct field change on the request-serving snapshot that drives watch termination.","risk_signals":["struct fields added","concurrency"],"focus":["Snapshot entries with nil watches are handled by serveWatch"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/router.go:NewGrafanaRouter","summary":"The router creates a root watches context at construction, to be cancelled by closeWatches at service stop.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"Router creates a root context for ending all watches","confidence":0.65,"reason":"Adds lifecycle context affecting shutdown behavior of long-running requests.","risk_signals":["shutdown behavior","context lifecycle"],"focus":["closeWatches cancels this context, and routers built elsewhere are not left with a nil one"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/router.go:(*GrafanaRouter).reconcile","summary":"A changed key now replaces the handlerEntry instead of mutating it, and replaced or removed entries have their watches cancelled after publish. Clients with open watches are disconnected on backend changes and must re-watch.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"Reconcile replaces changed entries and ends their watches after publish","confidence":0.85,"reason":"Changes handler replacement semantics and terminates live client connections on backend change.","risk_signals":["concurrency","connections terminated on reconcile","state replacement"],"focus":["Cancellation after publish lets re-watching clients reach the new backend","Early returns before the cancel loop do not leak retired entries","Breaker reset for changed keys and preservation for unchanged keys still hold"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/router.go:(*GrafanaRouter).publish","summary":"The published snapshot entry now includes the handler's watches context so requests can bind watches to the current handler generation.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"Published snapshot entries now include the watches context","confidence":0.7,"reason":"Feeds the context that ends watches into the serving snapshot; wrong wiring would leave watches uncancelled.","risk_signals":["concurrency","snapshot contents"],"focus":["watches is propagated for every entry, including ones with a nil backend"]}
]}
